---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const optCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · A call option: the right, not the duty, to BUY', note: 'At strike K, before expiry. If price rises above K, profit is price − K − premium. If not, you lose only the premium. Upside unlimited, downside capped at what you paid.' },
    { math: '2 · A put option: the right to SELL', note: 'Insurance for holders: if the price crashes below K, the put pays the difference. Portfolio protection — the premium is the insurance cost.' },
    { math: '3 · Payoff shapes', note: 'Stock: straight line through zero. Call: flat then kinked upward. Put: flat then kinked downward. Options are Lego for payoff shapes — any view (up, down, sideways, volatile) can be built.' },
    { math: '4 · Pricing: Black–Scholes (1973)', note: 'Value depends on price, strike, time, volatility and rates. Volatility is the only unknown input — which is why "implied volatility" is its own market (the VIX, the "fear index"). Nobel 1997.' },
  ],
};

const payoffPlot = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'max(0, x - 100) - 5', color: '#1d4ed8', label: 'long call, K=100, premium 5' },
    { expr: '5 - max(0, x - 100)', color: '#b42318', label: 'short call, K=100, premium 5' },
    { expr: 'max(0, 100 - x) - 5', color: '#0f766e', label: 'long put, K=100, premium 5' },
  ],
  x: { min: 70, max: 130, label: 'underlying price at expiry' },
  y: { min: -25, max: 25, label: 'profit / loss' },
  height: 320,
};

const odForwardEstimate = {
  "prompt": "Cash-and-carry: a stock trades at £100 and interest rates are 5% for one year. What is the fair one-year forward price, F = S·e^rT?",
  "unit": "£",
  "answer": 105.13,
  "factor": 1.15,
  "why": "F = 100·e^0.05 ≈ 105.13 — exactly the cost of buying the stock now and carrying it, financed at 5%, to delivery. Any deviation is free money, which is why forward prices are arithmetic rather than forecasts.",
  "context": "If F is higher: sell the forward, buy the stock with borrowed funds. If lower, do the reverse."
};

const odStrategyMatch = {
  "prompt": "Lego for payoff shapes — pair each position with the view it expresses:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "Long call",
      "right": "a leveraged bull position with capped downside"
    },
    {
      "left": "Long put",
      "right": "portfolio insurance — a floor under held shares"
    },
    {
      "left": "Straddle",
      "right": "a bet on volatility, not direction"
    },
    {
      "left": "Collar",
      "right": "insurance paid for by selling upside"
    },
    {
      "left": "Spread",
      "right": "limits both sides of the payoff"
    }
  ],
  "why": "\"What is your payoff diagram?\" is the first professional question in any options conversation — and portfolio insurance's own 1987 crash story is the cautionary footnote."
};

const odBreakevenSpot = {
  "prompt": "One line of this options-desk note is wrong — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "Exercising a call above the strike is rational.",
    "The premium was paid up front, before expiry.",
    "Breakeven sits exactly at the strike.",
    "A fund holding shares plus index puts has bought a floor.",
    "Straddles bet on volatility rather than direction."
  ],
  "errorLine": 2,
  "correction": "Breakeven is strike plus premium — with the stock at 102, a call struck at 100 is exercised anyway and still nets a loss of 3.",
  "why": "In-the-money and profitable are different claims: the premium must be earned back first, and it is the market's no-arbitrage price of the payoff's time value."
};

const odParityCloze = {
  "text": "Put-call parity: C − P = S − K·e^(−rT) — a long call plus a short %%0%% replicates a forward. With S = 100, K = 100 and r = 5% over one year, the right side is 100 − 95.12 = %%1%%.",
  "answers": [
    [
      "put"
    ],
    [
      "4.88",
      "£4.88"
    ]
  ],
  "hint": "Long call + short put = long forward; 100·e^−0.05 ≈ 95.12."
};

const odSmilePlace = {
  "prompt": "The volatility smile: where is deep out-of-the-money crash insurance priced, relative to the lognormal model's prediction?",
  "labels": [
    "far below",
    "below",
    "in line",
    "above",
    "far above"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 100
  },
  "answer": 75,
  "tolerance": 12,
  "why": "Systematically above — the smile/skew, a permanent scar from 1987 — so traders price volatility surfaces, not scalars, and implied volatility becomes a market of its own (the VIX, computed from index-option prices)."
};

const odSwapBranch = {
  "root": "You are treasurer. The company pays floating rates on £100m of debt and fears rises.",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "text": "The bank quotes an interest-rate swap. Which leg do you pay?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Pay 3.5% fixed, receive the floating index",
          "next": "test",
          "feedback": "Fixed-for-floating: the classic hedge for a floating-rate borrower."
        },
        {
          "label": "Receive 3.5% fixed, pay floating",
          "next": "test",
          "feedback": "That is the other side's trade — it would double your exposure to rising rates."
        },
        {
          "label": "No swap — keep the floating exposure",
          "next": "test",
          "feedback": "A choice, but not a hedge: every rise in rates lands directly on the interest bill."
        }
      ]
    },
    "test": {
      "text": "Over the swap's life, floating averages 4.5%. What is your effective borrowing cost?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "3.5% — the floating leg pays you 1% back",
          "next": "flip",
          "feedback": "Exactly: receiving 4.5% while paying 3.5% fixed nets +1%, offsetting the rise."
        },
        {
          "label": "4.5% — the swap cannot help",
          "next": "flip",
          "feedback": "It can: the swap nets +1% against the 3.5% fixed leg."
        }
      ]
    },
    "flip": {
      "text": "Now floating averages 2.5%. Effective cost?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "label": "Still 3.5% — you pay the 1% difference away",
          "next": "end",
          "feedback": "The hedge holds in both directions: rates fell, the swap cost you 1%, the budget never moved."
        },
        {
          "label": "2.5% — you pocket the cheap rates",
          "next": "end",
          "feedback": "No — paying 3.5% fixed against 2.5% floating nets −1% this time. Effective cost unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    "end": {
      "text": "Either way the cost is 3.5%: banks intermediate by quoting the fixed leg, and this swap market is the largest in the world by notional."
    }
  },
  "why": "A swap exchanges payment streams — fixed-for-floating on a notional principal — turning uncertain financing costs into a budgeted one."
};

const odGreekMatch = {
  "prompt": "The risk dashboard has five dials — pair each Greek with what it measures:",
  "pairs": [
    {
      "left": "Delta",
      "right": "price change per unit of underlying — runs 0 to 1, about 0.5 at-the-money"
    },
    {
      "left": "Gamma",
      "right": "how fast delta itself changes — a short-gamma hedger buys high and sells low"
    },
    {
      "left": "Theta",
      "right": "time decay — accelerating in the final weeks for near-the-money strikes"
    },
    {
      "left": "Vega",
      "right": "sensitivity to implied volatility — a spike pays the holder regardless of direction"
    },
    {
      "left": "Rho",
      "right": "interest-rate sensitivity — minor for equity options, central for long-dated rates products"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Risk limits are set per Greek because each dimension kills differently — direction, acceleration, decay, volatility — and desks keep smile-adjusted Greeks, stress-testing beyond the model."
};

const odRetailPredict = {
  "q": "You buy one call, strike £100, premium £5. At expiry the stock is £102 and you exercise. Your net result?",
  "choices": [
    "+£2 — the option finished in the money",
    "−£3 — the premium eats the gain",
    "£0 — exercising cancels the premium"
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "why": "Exercising is rational above the strike, but the £5 premium was paid up front: breakeven is strike plus premium, so £102 nets −£3. In-the-money is not the same as profitable — and most retail options expire worthless besides."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/advanced/options-and-derivatives">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced lesson in Finance. It builds on the foundational lesson Compound
    Interest and the Time Value of Money and the intermediate lessons Stocks &amp; Bonds,
    Time Value of Money (NPV) and Diversification &amp; Portfolios: discounting a future
    payoff, valuing an asset and thinking in portfolios are precisely the moves that
    derivatives pricing repurposes. Everything else is built here from one principle,
    no-arbitrage.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>frame a derivative's economics as risk relocation rather than prediction;</li>
    <li>price a forward by cash-and-carry arbitrage, and say why the answer is arithmetic, not forecast;</li>
    <li>read any option payoff diagram and point to the capped side and the uncapped side;</li>
    <li>use put-call parity to detect — and value — a mispricing;</li>
    <li>name the Greeks and say which risk each measures.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A derivative is a contract whose value <strong>derives</strong> from something else — a share,
    an interest rate, a commodity, a default. The family is old (forwards on rice in 18th-century
    Osaka, tulip options in 1637) and enormous today: notional values in the hundreds of trillions
    against a world economy near a tenth of that. The economics of derivatives is the economics of
    <strong>risk relocation</strong>: they let a farmer, an airline, a pension fund and a hedge
    fund trade the risks they each hold for the risks they would rather have — with a pricing
    theory (no-arbitrage) precise enough that a formula from 1973 still anchors a multi-trillion
    market.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="calls, puts, payoff shapes, and the pricing formula" widget="equationStep" config={optCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> step through the four cards in order: the call and put
    definitions hand you the asymmetry (a right, never a duty), the payoff-shapes card turns
    it into geometry, and the pricing card names the one input nobody observes — volatility.
    At each step ask who holds the option and who the obligation; that division drives
    everything that follows.
  </p>

  <h2>Forwards and futures: price certainty by contract</h2>
  <p>
    The simplest derivative is a <strong>forward</strong>: an agreement today to buy or sell an
    asset at a fixed price on a fixed date. Its logic is arbitrage, not opinion: the fair forward
    price of a stock trading at S with interest rate r over time T is F = S·e<sup>rT</sup> —
    exactly the cost of buying the stock now and carrying it (financed at r) to the delivery date.
    Any deviation creates free money: if F is too high, sell the forward and buy the stock with
    borrowed funds; if too low, do the reverse — and the trading itself closes the gap. This is
    <strong>cash-and-carry arbitrage</strong>, and it is why forward prices are arithmetic rather
    than forecasts. <strong>Futures</strong> are forwards standardised and exchange-traded, with
    the innovation that makes scale possible: a <strong>clearinghouse</strong> standing between
    every buyer and seller, and <strong>daily margin</strong> — gains and losses settled in cash
    every day, so no one accumulates unsecured exposure. <strong>Worked example:</strong> a wheat
    farmer expects harvest in September, fears falling prices, and sells futures at $6.50/bushel.
    If harvest-time prices fall to $5.50, the crop sells for less but the futures position gains
    $1.00 — the farmer's revenue is locked. If prices rise to $7.50, the crop gains $1.00 but the
    futures lose $1.00 — same revenue. The hedge eliminates price risk (not quantity or basis
    risk — the local-vs-futures gap that hedgers still wear) and transfers it to whoever chose to
    hold it, paid to do so.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="call the carry — price the forward" widget="estimate" config={odForwardEstimate} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> forwards and futures relocate price risk by contract, and
    arbitrage — not opinion — fixes their prices. Next come contracts that relocate risk
    asymmetrically: one side buys a right, the other sells an obligation.
  </p>

  <h2>Options: asymmetric payoffs</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>call</strong> grants the right (not the duty) to buy at strike K before expiry; a
    <strong>put</strong> the right to sell. The asymmetry is the product: a stock's payoff is a
    line through zero; an option's is a <em>hockey stick</em> — capped loss (the premium) on one
    side, unhinged upside on the other. <strong>Worked example:</strong> a call on a stock at
    £100, strike £100, premium £5. At expiry with the stock at £120: exercise and profit £15
    (£20 intrinsic minus the £5 paid) — a 300% return on a 20% stock move. With the stock at
    £102: exercise anyway, net −£3. At £98: walk away, lose the £5 premium, no more. The buyer's
    loss is strictly capped at the premium; the seller's is not capped at all — which is why
    option selling is a business for institutions with pricing and risk machinery, and why
    retail sellers of naked options occasionally generate famous blow-ups. The premium itself
    decomposes into <strong>intrinsic value</strong> (payoff if exercised now: max(0, S − K) for
    calls) and <strong>time value</strong> (the value of what might still happen) — the second
    being where option pricing lives.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="expiry payoff diagrams — drag nothing, read the asymmetry: capped loss below, line above" widget="plotter" config={payoffPlot} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> read vertical distance as money won or lost at expiry.
    Below the strike the long call loses exactly its 5-point premium, while the short call —
    its mirror image — collects that premium on the way down but bleeds without limit above.
    The long put pays only once the price is below the strike by more than the premium:
    insurance with a deductible.
  </p>

  <p>
    Read the figure and the strategy language writes itself. The long call is a leveraged bull
    position with capped downside; the short call collects premium and bears unlimited upside
    risk; the long put is portfolio insurance (a fund holding shares plus index puts has bought a
    floor — the strategy that produced portfolio-insurance's own 1987 crash story). Combinations
    build any view: straddles (call + put, same strike — a bet on volatility, not direction),
    collars (long put funded by short call — insurance paid for by selling upside), spreads
    (limit both sides). Options are Lego for payoff functions; "what is your payoff diagram?" is
    the first professional question in any options conversation.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each position with its view" widget="match" config={odStrategyMatch} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "if the option finishes in the money, I made money."
    Exercising above the strike is rational — but the premium was paid up front, so
    breakeven sits at strike plus premium. The lesson's own example: with the stock at 102,
    a call struck at 100 is exercised anyway and still nets a loss. A cheap premium is not
    a bargain either: it is the market's no-arbitrage price of the payoff's time value.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="find the faulty line — the breakeven brief" widget="spot-error" config={odBreakevenSpot} />

  <h2>Put-call parity and the no-arbitrage discipline</h2>
  <p>
    The deepest idea in derivatives is <strong>no-arbitrage pricing</strong>: prices must be
    consistent enough that no riskless profit exists, and consistency relations follow. The
    cleanest is <strong>put-call parity</strong> for European options: C − P = S − K·e<sup>−rT</sup>
    — long call plus short put replicates a forward. <strong>Worked example:</strong> S = £100,
    K = £100, r = 5%, T = 1 year: the right side is 100 − 100·e<sup>−0.05</sup> ≈ 100 − 95.12 =
    £4.88. If the call trades at £8 and the put at £2 (parity violated by £1.12), the arbitrage
    is mechanical: buy the put, sell the call, buy the stock, borrow — collect £1.12 per share
    risklessly; the trading collapses the mispricing in minutes. Parity violations are rare and
    small precisely because thousands of desks run this arithmetic continuously — arbitrage is
    the market's immune system, and option theory is its antibody design.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="fill the parity identity" widget="cloze" config={odParityCloze} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> no-arbitrage prices a forward outright and shackles a call to its
    matching put through parity. One gap remains — pricing an option on its own — and the same
    discipline, pushed to full power, closes it.
  </p>

  <h2>Black–Scholes: pricing the time value</h2>
  <p>
    Black, Scholes and Merton (1973) priced the option by an argument that looks like magic and
    is actually the no-arbitrage logic at full power: a position in the underlying stock,
    <em>continuously rebalanced</em> (delta hedged), can replicate the option's payoff exactly —
    and anything replicable must price at its replication cost, whatever investors believe about
    direction. The formula needs the stock price, strike, time, interest rate and
    <strong>volatility</strong> — the only genuinely unknown input. Three consequences organise
    modern markets. First, <strong>implied volatility</strong>: invert the formula, and an
    option's market price becomes a volatility forecast — the VIX ("fear index") is exactly this,
    computed from index-option prices. Second, the <strong>Greeks</strong>: sensitivities
    (delta to price, gamma to delta's change, theta to time decay, vega to volatility) are the
    risk dashboard every trading desk manages daily; market-makers selling options are
    delta-hedgers earning premium against hedging costs. Third, the theory's imperfections are
    their own market: real prices imply different volatilities at different strikes (the
    <strong>volatility smile/skew</strong> — crash insurance is systematically priced above the
    lognormal model's prediction, a permanent scar from 1987), so traders price surfaces, not
    scalars. Nobel 1997 (Scholes and Merton); Black died before eligibility.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pin the smile — where crash insurance sits" widget="place" config={odSmilePlace} />

  <h2>Swaps, credit derivatives, and the systemic lesson</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Swaps</strong> exchange payment streams — the workhorse being the interest-rate
    swap: fixed-for-floating on a notional principal. <strong>Worked example:</strong> a company
    pays floating rates on £100m of debt and fears rises; it enters a swap paying 3.5% fixed,
    receiving the floating index. If floating averages 4.5%, the swap nets it +1% — effective
    borrowing cost 3.5%; if floating averages 2.5%, it pays the difference — same 3.5%. The swap
    converts uncertainty into a chosen price of certainty; banks intermediate by quoting the
    fixed leg, and the interest-rate swap market is the largest in the world by notional.
    <strong>Credit default swaps</strong> transfer default risk: the buyer pays a spread, the
    seller pays on a credit event — insurance in economics, but written as contracts, which
    mattered enormously in 2008: CDS written on mortgage-backed securities (AIG's book) created
    correlated, undercollateralised promises that no one had stress-tested; individually sensible
    positions became a systemic knot through opacity, chain-linkage and leverage. The post-2008
    reforms (central clearing, margin, trade repositories) are engineering responses to exactly
    that: the same notional plumbing, drained of the parts that fail silently.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="run the swap through both rate worlds" widget="branch" config={odSwapBranch} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "derivatives are gambling, full stop." The same instrument is
    insurance in one portfolio and speculation in another — the economic function lives in the
    position, not the contract. A put bought against a held share portfolio is insurance; the
    identical put bought naked is a bet. Regulation, accounting and the 2008 lesson all organise
    around this distinction; dismissing the instrument (or romanticising it) misses the actual
    question, which is who bears which risk, with what visibility and margin.
  </p>

  <h2>The Greeks: the risk dashboard</h2>
  <p>
    Professional option risk is managed through the sensitivities. <strong>Delta</strong>
    (∂V/∂S): the option's price change per unit of underlying — a call's delta runs from 0 to
    1 (at-the-money around 0.5), and delta-hedging (holding delta shares against the option)
    is the market-maker's neutralising trade. <strong>Gamma</strong> (∂Δ/∂S): how fast delta
    itself changes — highest at-the-money and near expiry, and gamma's costs explain the
    smile: a short-gamma hedger must buy high and sell low as the market moves (the
    volatility premium's microstructure). <strong>Theta</strong>: time decay — options are
    wasting assets, with accelerating decay in the final weeks for near-the-money strikes.
    <strong>Vega</strong>: sensitivity to implied volatility — long options are long
    volatility, and a volatility spike pays the holder regardless of direction.
    <strong>Rho</strong>: interest-rate sensitivity, minor for equity options, central for
    long-dated rates products. The portfolio view: every options book is a vector of Greeks,
    and risk limits are set per Greek because each dimension kills differently — direction
    (delta), acceleration (gamma), decay (theta), volatility (vega). The Black–Scholes
    assumptions underlie all of them, which is why desks keep smile-adjusted Greeks and
    stress-test beyond the model: the dashboard reads the model, the book lives in the
    market.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="pair each Greek with its dial" widget="match" config={odGreekMatch} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a delta-hedged position is a safe position." Neutralising
    delta removes only the first-order, directional risk — the dashboard has more dials. As
    the price moves, delta itself drifts (gamma), forcing the hedger to buy high and sell
    low; time decay (theta) charges every day; a volatility spike reprices the book (vega).
    This is why desks set limits per Greek: each dimension kills differently, and the
    hedging flows themselves move the market.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A wheat farmer sells September futures; an airline buys them. Neither is guessing about the weather. What economic function is the futures market performing for both?</li>
    <li>A call trades at USD 8 and the put with the same strike and expiry at USD 2; the stock is at USD 100 and the discounted strike is USD 95.12. What trade can you put on, and why will the opportunity not last?</li>
    <li>Why does the replication argument make the option's price independent of anyone's belief about the stock's direction?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Risk relocation: the farmer holds the wheat price as a
    risk and trades it away; the airline holds the opposite exposure and pays to take it on.
    (2) Parity requires
    call minus put to equal stock minus discounted strike — 4.88 here, against a market 6.00:
    buy the put, sell the call, buy the stock, borrow, and collect about USD 1.12 per share
    risklessly; thousands of desks run this arithmetic continuously, so the gap collapses in
    minutes. (3) Because a rebalanced stock-and-borrowing position reproduces the payoff
    exactly, and anything replicable must price at its replication cost — a directional
    belief would create a second, conflicting price, and the gap between them is the
    arbitrage.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A derivative is a contract whose value derives from an underlying; its economics is risk relocation, not prediction.</li>
    <li>Forward and futures prices are set by cash-and-carry arbitrage — arithmetic, not forecasts.</li>
    <li>An option's asymmetry is the product: the buyer's loss is capped at the premium; the seller's is not capped at all.</li>
    <li>Put-call parity (C − P = S − K·e<sup>−rT</sup>) turns internal inconsistency into free money for whoever spots a violation.</li>
    <li>Black–Scholes prices the time value by replication; volatility is the only input the market does not hand you, which is why implied volatility is its own market.</li>
    <li>The Greeks are the risk dashboard: delta, gamma, theta, vega and rho measure different ways a book can lose.</li>
    <li>The same contract is insurance in one portfolio and a bet in another — the position, not the instrument, is the economics.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The same no-arbitrage machinery runs through this subject's other advanced lessons:
    Portfolio Theory (Markowitz) asks how to combine risky assets into the best portfolio,
    and Corporate Finance turns discount rates and option-like payoffs on whole firms.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Risk-neutral valuation, properly stated.</strong> The replication argument implies
    that options can be priced as the discounted expected payoff under an artificial
    "risk-neutral" probability measure — no utility functions, no risk premia, because replication
    deletes the risk. The binomial (Cox–Ross–Rubinstein) model makes the machinery visible with
    two branches per step and converges to Black–Scholes; it remains the workhorse for American
    options (where early exercise breaks closed forms) and exotic payoffs.
    <strong>The empirical layer.</strong> Real markets violate the model's assumptions
    measurably: fat-tailed returns (Mandelbrot's critique, then Mandelbrot vindicated), jumps,
    stochastic volatility — the whole "smile" literature is a quarter-century of model repair,
    with Heston-type stochastic-volatility and local-vol models as the practical standard.
    <strong>Open questions:</strong> the explosion of zero-days-to-expiry (0DTE) index options
    now a large share of US equity-options volume — what they do to underlying volatility and
    end-of-day hedging flows is unsettled and monitored by regulators; volatility's own term
    structure and the risk-premium embedded in variance swaps; the model risk of correlation
    (the 2008 CDO lesson: pricing multi-name credit is correlation pricing, and correlation is
    the parameter that moves when everything else does); and where the line between hedging and
    systemic fragility sits when the hedgers are the market — the dynamic-hedging feedback
    (sell into falling markets to stay delta-neutral) that portfolio insurance dramatised in
    1987 and that modern vol-targeting funds re-enact at larger scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    The retail dimension deserves honesty on both sides. Options offer defined-risk
    structures — buying an option caps loss at the premium, an asymmetry equity leverage
    cannot match — and covered-call and collar strategies are legitimate income and insurance
    tools. Against that: most retail options expire worthless, the leverage asymmetry
    destroys small accounts quickly, and the platform gamification of options trading (the
    2020–2021 retail boom's documentation) is a public-health-grade case study in product
    design meeting behavioural bias. The professional framing is unchanged by any of it: the
    instrument transfers risk; the questions are always who is being paid to hold it, with
    what capital, under what model — and whether the person on the other side of the screen
    knows which of those roles they have taken.
  </p>
